---
import CaseStudyLayout from "@/layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro";
import SectionHeading from "@/components/SectionHeading.astro";
import VideoFigure from "@/components/VideoFigure.astro";

const tocItems = [
  { id: "the-before-and-after", label: "The before and after" },
  { id: "the-design-loop", label: "The design loop" },
  { id: "key-decisions", label: "Key decisions that made it work" },
  { id: "what-changed", label: "What changed" },
  { id: "reflection", label: "Reflection" },
];
---

<CaseStudyLayout
  title="Bizwise: The Backbone for Small Service Businesses"
  description="A product design case study: turning static, scattered contact lists into a living customer relationship workspace for small and medium-sized service businesses."
  date="2021-05-23"
  dateFormatted="May 23, 2021"
  tocItems={tocItems}
>
  <p>
    I'm excited to share the story of Bizwise. Between May and August 2021, I led the
    design of a customer list management system that turned static, scattered contact
    lists into a living workspace.
  </p>

  <p>
    What started as a response to the everyday chaos of small and medium-sized service
    businesses became something more fundamental: the quiet system of record that kept
    their customer relationships intact.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Role:</strong> Lead Product Designer (end-to-end: research, systems
    thinking, UX/UI, usability testing, and iterative refinement with real users).
  </p>

  <p><strong>Timeline:</strong> May to August 2021.</p>

  <SectionHeading id="the-before-and-after" title="The before and after" />

  <p>
    The best way to explain it is to show the before-and-after. Most SMBs I talked to
    were running their entire customer world across spreadsheets, email contacts, paper
    lists, and lightweight tools that never quite talked to each other. Data lived in
    fragments. Follow-ups got missed. Activity history disappeared. Employees sometimes
    walked out with the client list.
  </p>

  <p>
    Existing payment or scheduling platforms solved pieces of the workflow, but none of
    them gave non-technical owners a reliable place to remember and nurture relationships.
    Bizwise closed that gap.
  </p>

  <p>
    Research came from forum analysis, interviews, and competitor teardowns. The numbers
    were stark: fewer than 5% of these businesses used a full CRM. Almost everyone lived
    in spreadsheets or inboxes. What they actually wanted was simple and powerful at the
    same time:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Automatic customer creation from real interactions (forms, emails, calls)</li>
    <li>A clear activity timeline</li>
    <li>Easy grouping, tagging, notes, and file attachments</li>
    <li>Reminders for birthdays, anniversaries, and next steps</li>
    <li>Controlled employee access so client data couldn't walk out the door</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Those insights shaped every decision. We weren't building another sales CRM. We were
    building a relationship memory system for people who just wanted to run their
    business.
  </p>

  <VideoFigure
    src="/assets/images/Bizwise01.webm"
    caption="The new customer creation flow, turning a blank entry into a living profile in seconds."
  />

  <SectionHeading id="the-design-loop" title="The design loop" />

  <p>
    I approached it the same way I approach most product work: systems thinking first,
    then relentless iteration with real users. I mapped the core flows (customer creation
    and editing, activity tracking, grouping and tagging, contact import, and duplicate
    merging) then moved into low-fidelity prototypes.
  </p>

  <p>
    Two rounds of usability testing with actual small business owners refined the
    navigation, the grouping logic, and the discoverability of key actions.
  </p>

  <p>
    The biggest learning came early: importing from a spreadsheet had to feel almost
    effortless. Users expected it to be dead simple, so the final design centered on a
    guided import process that removed friction at the exact moment it mattered most.
  </p>

  <VideoFigure
    src="/assets/images/Bizwise02.webm"
    caption="The guided import process: spreadsheet uploads that feel effortless, not technical."
  />

  <SectionHeading id="key-decisions" title="Key decisions that made it work" />

  <h3>Automatic customer creation</h3>
  <p>
    Inbound interactions like website forms, emails, and calendar events could spin up a
    customer profile without manual data entry. Profiles appeared ready to use, already
    linked to the source activity.
  </p>

  <VideoFigure
    src="/assets/images/Bizwise03.webm"
    caption="Automatic profile creation: a new customer appears ready to use, linked to the interaction that created it."
  />

  <h3>Activity timeline</h3>
  <p>
    Every email, appointment, note, and file lived in one chronological view. Owners could
    finally see the relationship at a glance instead of hunting across tools.
  </p>

  <VideoFigure
    src="/assets/images/Bizwise04.webm"
    caption="The customer profile: every email, appointment, note, and file in one chronological view."
  />

  <h3>Smart grouping and tagging</h3>
  <p>
    Targeted outreach became practical. Tags and groups let businesses segment without
    complexity.
  </p>

  <VideoFigure
    src="/assets/images/Bizwise05.webm"
    caption="Grouping and merging: segment customers without complexity, merge duplicates cleanly."
  />

  <h3>File attachments and reminders</h3>
  <p>
    Invoices, appraisals, contracts, and personal details (birthdays, anniversaries) sat
    right on the profile. Relationship tracking stopped being an afterthought.
  </p>

  <h3>Access controls</h3>
  <p>
    Limited employee permissions addressed the real fear of customer poaching while still
    letting the team do its job.
  </p>

  <p>
    These pieces connected customer data to website forms, emails, calendars, and invoices.
    The architecture treated the customer list as the hub rather than another isolated
    spreadsheet.
  </p>

  <VideoFigure
    src="/assets/images/Bizwise06.webm"
    caption="The full customer creation experience: from empty state to a connected, living profile."
  />

  <SectionHeading id="what-changed" title="What changed" />

  <p>The metrics told the story clearly:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>38%</strong> faster customer onboarding</li>
    <li><strong>41%</strong> reduction in manual data entry</li>
    <li><strong>26%</strong> increase in repeat customer engagement</li>
    <li><strong>2.7×</strong> faster profile creation</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Behind the numbers was a quieter shift: small service businesses finally had a single,
    trustworthy place where customer relationships lived and grew.
  </p>

  <SectionHeading id="reflection" title="Reflection" />

  <p>
    This project reinforced something I keep coming back to: simplicity is often more
    valuable than feature depth. Traditional CRMs optimize for sales teams and pipeline
    velocity. Small businesses primarily need tools that help them remember people and
    nurture relationships without friction. Designing strictly around that insight produced
    a more focused product.
  </p>

  <p>
    The work also sharpened my skills in iterative, user-centered design and highlighted
    how critical seamless integrations and restrained feature sets are for non-technical
    owners. Feature overload is always a temptation in complex ecosystems; resisting it was
    one of the harder and more important parts of the process.
  </p>

  <p>
    Looking at it now, in an AI-driven landscape, the natural next layer would be
    auto-tagging and predictive relationship insights. But the foundation remains the same:
    give small businesses a reliable backbone for the relationships that keep them alive.
  </p>

  <blockquote>
    <p>
      Bizwise didn't try to be everything. It tried to be the dependable center of the
      customer relationship for the people who needed it most. That turned out to be
      enough.
    </p>
    <cite>Masudur Rahman</cite>
  </blockquote>

  <Fragment slot="footer">
    <h3 class="text-base font-semibold mt-0 mb-2">Key outcomes</h3>
    <ul class="mb-5">
      <li>Unified fragmented sources (spreadsheets, emails, paper) into one accessible system</li>
      <li>Automated profile creation and activity tracking that cut manual work</li>
      <li>Grouping, reminders, and controlled exports that supported better outreach and lower churn</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="mb-5">
      If you're working on tools for small businesses, or thinking about relationship-first
      product design, I'd love to hear from you.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</CaseStudyLayout>
